#{[17592186055013 "Implicit differentiation gives $2x+8y\\,y'=0$, so $y'=-x/(4y)$ wherever $y\\ne0$. At $(\\sqrt2,1)$, the slope is $$-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}=-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}.$$"] [17592186055014 "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0)$."] [17592186055017 "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=(0,\\tfrac{10}3)$."] [17592186055138 "Differentiate implicitly: $3x^2+3y^2y'=0$. Hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$$ wherever the curve has a finite derivative, namely $y\\ne0$."] [17592186055139 "Differentiate implicitly: $$-\\sin y\\,y'+4x-y'=10.$$ Solving for the derivative gives $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4x-10}{1+\\sin y}=\\frac{2(2x-5)}{\\sin y+1},$$ where the denominator is nonzero."] [17592186055140 "Apply the product and chain rules: $y+xy'+2yy'=6x$. Thus $$(x+2y)y'=6x-y\\implies\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6x-y}{x+2y},$$ where $x+2y\\ne0$."] [17592186055369 "The line has slope $2$ and vertical intercept $-4$. It passes through $(0,-4)$ and $(2,0)$, so choose the graph with these intercepts and positive slope $2$."] [17592186055429 "Substituting $x=0,1,2,3$ into $y=-x+1$ gives $y=1,0,-1,-2$. The required graph passes through these four points and slopes downward."] [17592186055430 "The equation $y=\\frac12x-2$ has vertical intercept $-2$ and horizontal intercept $4$. Choose the line through $(0,-2)$ and $(4,0)$."] [17592186059526 "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{a} + 6\\vec{b} &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059528 "We have\n\n$$\\overset{\\to}{OA} = a = i + 3j,; \\overset{\\to}{OB} = b = 2i - 2j$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\overset{\\to}{AB} &= \\overset{\\to}{OB} - \\overset{\\to}{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186059530 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186059543 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059544 "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059959 "The strict inequalities exclude both endpoints. Use parentheses at both ends: $x\\in(3,7)$."] [17592186060012 "The lower endpoint $9$ is excluded, and the upper endpoint $13$ is included. Thus $x\\in(9,13]$."] [17592186060025 "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle12,5\\rangle-\\langle9,-2\\rangle=\\langle12-9,5-(-2)\\rangle=\\langle3,7\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060028 "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle4,1\\rangle-\\langle1,-4\\rangle=\\langle3,5\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060029 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{4^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{17}.$$"] [17592186060030 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-21+18 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060031 "Multiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=(-1)(10)+(0)(2)+(6)(-5)=-10-30=-40.\n$$"] [17592186060041 "Multiply each component by $3$:\n$$3\\langle8,-7\\rangle=\\langle24,-21\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060046 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10-28)\\mathbf{i}-(-3-16)\\mathbf{j}+(-21-40)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-18\\mathbf{i}+19\\mathbf{j}-61\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060047 "The source asks for the cross product. Expanding its determinant gives\n$$\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v=\\left\\langle9(3)-6(-3),\\ 6(0)-\\frac13(3),\\ \\frac13(-3)-9(0)\\right\\rangle=\\langle45,-1,-1\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060071 "Expand the two squares. Their mixed terms cancel, so $$(\\sin x-\\cos x)^2+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=2\\sin^2 x+2\\cos^2 x=2.$$"] [17592186060072 "On the domain of the original expression, rationalize and simplify:\n$$\\frac{\\cos x}{1-\\sin x}-\\tan x=\\frac{1+\\sin x}{\\cos x}-\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}=\\frac1{\\cos x}=\\sec x.$$"] [17592186060118 "First scale, then add components:\n$$\\langle-2,1\\rangle+2\\langle4,-1\\rangle=\\langle-2+8,1-2\\rangle=\\langle6,-1\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060119 "First $a+3b=\\langle-1,0\\rangle+3\\langle3,-2\\rangle=\\langle8,-6\\rangle$. Its norm is $10$. Dividing by this positive norm preserves direction and gives the unit vector $\\langle4/5,-3/5\\rangle$."] [17592186060124 "Compute $2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c=\\langle6,6\\rangle-\\langle-24,12\\rangle=\\langle30,-6\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c)=\\langle4,-2\\rangle-\\langle30,-6\\rangle=\\langle-26,4\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060125 "Compute $2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c=\\langle8,2\\rangle+\\langle-5,10\\rangle=\\langle3,12\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c)=\\langle1,1\\rangle-\\langle3,12\\rangle=\\langle-2,-11\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060126 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|a\\|=\\sqrt{(-12)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{153}=3\\sqrt{17}.$$"] [17592186060127 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle3-(-4),0-1\\rangle=\\langle7,-1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{7^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2.$$"] [17592186060128 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle3-(-2),3-2\\rangle=\\langle5,1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{5^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{26}.$$"] [17592186060129 "The norm of $a$ is $\\sqrt{3^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{73}$. Dividing by this positive number preserves direction and gives $$\\frac{a}{\\|a\\|}=\\begin{bmatrix}3/\\sqrt{73}\\\\8/\\sqrt{73}\\end{bmatrix}.$$"] [17592186060184 "Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero. Thus\n\n$$\nu\\cdot v=x^2+(-2)(2)=x^2-4=0.\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=\\pm2$. Since $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$."] [17592186060185 "Since $a\\perp b$, their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x=3$."] [17592186060193 "The source condition is the magnitude of a cross product. Compute\n$$\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\langle-7x,11x,-5\\rangle.$$\nSquaring the magnitude equation gives $170x^2+25=705$, so $x^2=4$ and $x=\\pm2$."] [17592186060194 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6x-8)\\mathbf{j}+(0-2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-(6x-8),-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-(6x-8))^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36{x}^{2}-96x+64+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77} & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+77 & =13 \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+64 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186060226 "Use a common denominator and the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\cos\\theta+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\sec\\theta.$$"] [17592186060252 "The square bracket includes $-5$ and the parenthesis excludes $2\\sqrt2$. Therefore $-5\\le y<2\\sqrt2$."] [17592186060264 "The open circle at $-1$ excludes that endpoint, and the filled circle at $2$ includes that endpoint. The segment represents $x\\in(-1,2]$."] [17592186060267 "Both endpoint circles, at $1$ and $4$, are open. The segment represents $x\\in(1,4)$."] [17592186060310 "Both parentheses exclude their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in(3,4)$ means $3<x<4$."] [17592186060311 "Both square brackets include their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in[-2,1]$ means $-2\\le x\\le1$."] [17592186060343 "Where the original denominator is nonzero,\n$$\\cos^2\\theta\\left(1+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}\\right)=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$"] [17592186060344 "Factor out $\\cos\\theta$:\n$$\\cos^3\\theta+\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=\\cos\\theta.$$"] [17592186060345 "On the domain of the original fraction,\n$$\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta+\\sin^4\\theta}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\tan\\theta.$$"] [17592186060346 "For the original expression, $\\sin x\\ne0$. Factoring its denominator gives\n$$\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin^3x+\\sin x\\cos^2x}=\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin x(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)}=\\sin x.$$"] [17592186060349 "Factor and apply the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\sin^2x\\cos^3x+\\sin^4x\\cos x=\\sin^2x\\cos x(\\cos^2x+\\sin^2x)=\\sin^2x\\cos x.$$"] [17592186060392 "Collect the corresponding components:\n$$(7\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j)+(3\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)=10\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j.$$"] [17592186060393 "Distribute the scalar and collect components:\n$$2(4\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)+(6\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j)=14\\mathbf i.$$"] [17592186064712 "An antiderivative of $x^3$ is $x^4/4$. Thus\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}x^3\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_{-2}^{2}=4-4=0.$$"] [17592186064714 "Since $x\\ge0$ on the interval, $\\sqrt{x^5}=x^{5/2}$. Hence\n$$\\int_0^4\\sqrt{x^5}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac27x^{7/2}\\right]_0^4=\\frac27(128)=\\frac{256}{7}.$$"] [17592186064853 "Apply the power rule:\n$$\\int_1^2x^{-4}\\,dx=\\left[-\\frac1{3x^3}\\right]_1^2=-\\frac1{24}+\\frac13=\\frac7{24}.$$"] [17592186064855 "On this nonnegative interval, the integrand is $x^{3/5}$. Thus\n$$\\int_0^1\\sqrt[5]{x^3}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac58x^{8/5}\\right]_0^1=\\frac58.$$"] [17592186069083 "Use the slope formula: $$m=\\frac{3-5}{2-4}=\\frac{-2}{-2}=1.$$"] [17592186069195 "The intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{0-4}{2-0}=-2.$$"] [17592186069343 "The plotted line is horizontal: its $y$-coordinate stays constant as $x$ changes. Its rise is zero, so its slope is $0$."] [17592186069344 "The plotted line is vertical. Any two distinct points on it have zero horizontal change, so the slope would require division by zero. The slope is undefined."] [17592186069348 "The plotted points are $(1,0)$ and $(3,6)$. Thus $$m=\\frac{6-0}{3-1}=3.$$"] [17592186069451 "The intercepts are $(-4,0)$ and $(0,2)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{2-0}{0-(-4)}=\\frac12.$$"] [17592186069452 "The line falls as we move from left to right. Thus its rise is negative for a positive run, and its slope is negative."] [17592186069511 "The line rises as we move from left to right. Thus its rise and run have the same sign, and its slope is positive."] [17592186073446 "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\\qquad\\:\\:\\:\n\\begin{cases}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{cases}.$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"] [17592186073997 "The first equation gives $q=3p-9$. Substitute into the second equation: $$5p+4(3p-9)=32\\implies17p=68\\implies p=4.$$ Hence $q=3(4)-9=3$. Substitution into both original equations gives $-9$ and $32$, as required."] [17592186073998 "The second equation gives $b=-a$. Substitute into the first equation: $$-7a+2(-a)=18\\implies-9a=18\\implies a=-2.$$ Then $b=2$. Indeed, $-7(-2)+2(2)=18$ and $6(-2)+6(2)=0$."] [17592186074030 "Substitute $y=x+5$ into the first equation: $$x+5(x+5)=13\\implies6x=-12\\implies x=-2.$$ Then $y=-2+5=3$. Both original equations hold, so the ordered solution is $(-2,3)$."] [17592186074047 "From $5q+55=0$, we obtain $q=-11$. Substituting into the second equation gives $2p-33+3=0$, so $p=15$. Therefore $p-q=15-(-11)=26$."] [17592186074172 "The second equation gives $b=2+4a$. Substitute into the first equation: $3a+2(2+4a)=4$, so $11a=0$. Thus $a=0$, $b=2$, and $a-b=-2$."] [17592186083545 "At $t=-1$, the point is $(0,1)$, while $x'(t)=4t^3+1=-3$ and $y'(t)=2t=-2$. The tangent slope is $2/3$, so the normal slope is $-3/2$. The normal is $y=1-3x/2$; setting $y=0$ gives $x=2/3$."] [17592186083547 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{2em}{y}^{'}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083614 "Since $dx/dt=1$ and $dy/dt=3t^2$, we have $dy/dx=3t^2$. A horizontal tangent requires $dy/dx=0$, which gives $t=0$."] [17592186083913 "At $t=-1$ the point is $(3,-1)$. The tangent slope is\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5t^4}{2t-2}=-\\frac54.$$\nHence its equation is $y+1=-\\frac54(x-3)$."] [17592186088018 "The center is the midpoint of the diameter: $((-4+2)/2,(2+2)/2)=(-1,2)$. The diameter has length $6$, so the radius is $3$. Thus $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=9.$$"] [17592186088019 "The center is the midpoint of $(6,4)$ and $(-4,2)$, namely $(1,3)$. The squared diameter is $10^2+2^2=104$, so the squared radius is $104/4=26$. Hence $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=26.$$"] [17592186088020 "Eliminating the parameter gives\n$$(x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=9^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)=81.$$\nThis is a circle centered at $(-3,5)$ with radius $9$."] [17592186088021 "The standard counterclockwise circle of radius $8$ about the origin is $x=8\\cos t$, $y=8\\sin t$. At $t=0$ it starts at $(8,0)$, and its initial velocity points upward as required."] [17592186088022 "The parametrization $x=6\\cos t$, $y=6\\sin t$ satisfies $x^2+y^2=36$, giving the circle of radius $6$ about the origin."] [17592186088030 "The square root requires $t\\ge0$. Therefore $y=\\sqrt t\\ge0$ and $x=t(t+6)\\ge0$, so the curve lies in the first quadrant together with the origin."] [17592186088031 "Complete the square:\n$$x=t^2-4t=(t-2)^2-4\\ge-4.$$\nThe lower bound is attained at $t=2$."]}
